97|**The question.** The convention scores a passed pawn as ±BIG (BIG = 256), sums pawn components, and treats
98|the king group Z (the trébuchet) as the game 0. When does this give the chess result?
99|
100|**The answer, in one paragraph.** The convention gives the chess result when four conditions hold, together
101|with the axiom (A_Q) that a won promotion race is a won game (Theorem 1.14):
102|- (V1) the king group never interacts with the components, and nobody is ever stalemated;
103|- (V2) every "decisive event" really decides the game;
104|- (V3) every move inside Z is decisively punished;
105|- (V4) no two components carry threats of opposite sign, and BIG exceeds every tempo count.
106|
107|Only (V4) concerns BIG. Without it, +BIG and −BIG cancel and no value of BIG helps (Proposition 1.12).
108|The other three are chess facts.
109|- For a king move, (V2)+(V3) reduce to a local certificate: the punisher promotes within T moves against a
110|  defender who may pass, and the defender cannot promote within T moves elsewhere (Theorem 2.7). The
111|  counter-promotion half of the certificate holds in every non-terminal context exactly when T = 1
112|  (Proposition 2.10). Slower punishments make the zero context-dependent.
113|- When a king has a safe square to shuttle to, Z is not 0 but the loopy game `on`/`off` (or `dud`). On that
114|  side BIG is meaningless, because `on` exceeds every number. The correct rule replaces ±BIG by
115|  game-ending outcomes (Theorem 3.4):
116|  - **if only White can pass,** Black wins iff Black can force a decisive event against a White who may
117|    pass at any time; otherwise White wins;
118|  - **if both sides can pass,** a side wins iff it can force a decisive event against a passing opponent;
119|    otherwise the game is drawn.
120|
121|**Results** (section number, label, one line each):
122|
123|| # | Statement | Label |
124||---|---|---|
125|| L1.6 | Adding options for a player never lowers that player's result | PROVED |
126|| L1.7 | Under normal play a move to the opponent's win can be deleted; under chess rules it cannot, because "no move" is stalemate | PROVED |
127|| T1.9 | One component: o(A^B) is the decisive-event outcome for every B ≥ 1 | PROVED |
128|| T1.11 | Sums: o(S) = decisive-event outcome under first-event dominance; sufficient: no opposite-sign threats in different components, and B > every tempo count | PROVED |
129|| P1.12 | {⊕ \| ∅} ⊞ {∅ \| ⊖} is 𝓝 in chess semantics, but its BIG translation is 0 (𝓟) for every B | PROVED |
130|| P1.13 | B must exceed every tempo count: with n tempi against a threat, the BIG outcome is right iff n ≤ B | PROVED |
131|| T1.14 | Validity: (V1)–(V3) give P = D (no draws), (V4) gives o(S) = P, and (A_Q) gives C = P | PROVED (C part modulo A_Q) |
132|| T2.3 | {−B \| B} = 0 | PROVED (and KNOWN: simplicity theorem) |
133|| T2.4 | Freeze theorem: if Z-moves are punished at every position where the mover is losing, then G + Z has the result of G | PROVED |
134|| T2.7 | Local certificate LC_T (local forced promotion against a passing defender, no faster counter-promotion, no stalemate) ⇒ decisive punishment | PROVED (in P) |
