196|**Definition 1.5 (four models of a king-and-pawn position X).**
197|- **C(X), chess.** The rules of `kings/SPEC.md`: checkmate wins, stalemate is a draw, infinite play is a draw.
198|- **P(X), the promotion model.** Chess, except that the first promotion ends the game as a win for the
199|  promoter. Stalemate and infinite play are still draws.
200|  - `kpsolve --frozen` computes P with frozen kings: a king move loses at once.
201|- **D(X), the decisive-event model.** A D-game. Its positions are the positions before the first decisive
202|  event. Its terminals are the decisive events:
203|  - a passer appears (as in `cylcgt`), with races scored by a race rule;
204|  - a punished move in the king group (§2).
205|- **S(X), the BIG convention.** The CGT game D(X)^B, with B = BIG and the components summed. This is
206|  `cylcgt.pawns.value`.
207|
208|The models are connected by bridges, each with its own hypotheses:
209|
210|```
211|S  --Thm 1.11 (V4)-->  D  --Thm 1.14 (V1-V3)-->  P  --(A_Q)-->  C
212|```
213|
214|**Axiom (A_Q) (CONJECTURE, checked per position).** If X is a P-win for σ, then X is a C-win for σ.
215|- *Evidence for:* the cylinder KQK tables agree in two independent solvers ([RESULTS.md](../../RESULTS.md) §2:
216|  KQK is 100% won on the 8×8 cylinder).
217|- *Evidence against, in general:* L4.4 of `notes/lemmas.md` is a promotion race lost to a promotion with check.
218|- *Settled by:* kpsolve with `--cap 1` computes C exactly whenever its bounds agree (A = B).
219|
220|### 1.3 Two lemmas about options
...
260|- "Left wins moving first" holds iff some Left option is won by Left with Right to move, in both games.
261|  The same holds for Right.
262|- A player with no option loses in both games. ∎
263|
264|In one component the convention cannot go wrong: after the first ±B nothing else happens. Trouble needs at
265|least two components (compare L2.3 of `notes/lemmas.md`).
266|
267|**Definition 1.10 (tempo counts, threats, dominance).** Let Y = (y₁, …, y_k) be a position of a sum of short
268|D-games.
269|- **Tempo counts.** M_L(y) is the largest number of Left moves in any sequence of moves from y (moves by
270|  both sides, in any order, not necessarily alternating), counting a final move into a terminal. M_R(y) is
271|  defined the same way for Right. Both are monotone: M(y′) ≤ M(y) whenever y′ is reachable from y.
272|  - In the pawn game every White move advances a White pawn by at least one rank, so
273|    M_W ≤ Σ_white pawns (H − rank) ≤ (H − 2)p for p legal White pawns. That is ≤ 6p ≤ 48 on 8 ranks.
274|- **Threats.** τ(y) ⊆ {+, −} contains + iff some position reachable from y without passing a terminal has a
275|  Left option ⊕. It contains − likewise for ⊖. Like M, τ is monotone.
276|- **First-event dominance.** Y is *first-event dominant for B* if the following holds for every Y′ reachable
277|  from Y without passing a terminal, and every option y′_j → σ (σ ∈ {⊕, ⊖}, by either player):
278|  - the CGT position σB + Σ_{i≠j} (y′_i)^B is won by σ's player whoever moves first;
279|  - here σB means +B if σ = ⊕ and −B if σ = ⊖.
